-- ScholarMesh -- 08d -- seed notes: Computer Networks + Operating Systems
INSERT INTO DOCUMENT (title, description, file_path, file_type, file_size_bytes, visibility, content, student_id, course_id, upload_date)
SELECT v.title, v.description, v.file_path, 'md', octet_length(v.content), v.visibility, v.content,
       s.student_id, c.course_id, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP - v.age
FROM (VALUES
  ('TCP vs UDP and Congestion Control'::varchar, 'Compares TCP and UDP, then walks through flow control, slow start, AIMD and fast retransmit.'::varchar, 'seed/tcp-udp-congestion.md'::varchar, 'public'::varchar, 'rahul.verma@scholarmesh.test'::varchar, 'UE23CS341A'::varchar, INTERVAL '37 days', $n$TCP and UDP are the two main transport-layer protocols. TCP is connection-oriented: before data moves, the hosts do a three-way handshake. The client sends SYN, the server replies SYN-ACK, and the client answers ACK. UDP is connectionless: it adds a tiny header (ports, length, checksum) and sends the datagram with no setup, ordering or delivery guarantee.

TCP gets reliability from sequence numbers, acknowledgements and retransmission. Every byte is numbered, the receiver ACKs what has arrived in order, and the sender keeps a timer. If no ACK comes before the timeout, the segment is sent again. Out-of-order segments are reordered before reaching the application.

Flow control and congestion control are easy to mix up. Flow control protects the receiver, which advertises a window (rwnd) saying how much buffer space is left. Congestion control protects the network: the sender keeps its own congestion window (cwnd) and never has more than min(rwnd, cwnd) bytes unacknowledged.

Congestion control works in phases. Slow start begins with cwnd at 1 MSS and doubles it every round trip until it reaches ssthresh. Congestion avoidance then adds about one MSS per round trip (additive increase). On loss, cwnd is halved (multiplicative decrease); together this is AIMD, giving the sawtooth graph. Three duplicate ACKs trigger fast retransmit, resending the missing segment without waiting for the timeout. A real timeout is more serious: cwnd drops to 1 and slow start restarts.

UDP is better when late data is worse than lost data, or when the app wants its own control. Live video calls, online games and DNS queries are typical: a retransmitted voice packet would arrive too late to play, so TCP's reliability would only add delay.$n$::text),
  ('OSI vs TCP/IP Layer Models', 'Lines up the seven OSI layers against the TCP/IP model with example protocols and how encapsulation works.', 'seed/osi-vs-tcpip.md', 'public', 'arjun.mehta@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS341A', INTERVAL '29 days', $n$The OSI model splits networking into seven layers, each solving one problem and using the services of the layer below it. From the top: Application (what the user program needs, like HTTP or SMTP), Presentation (data format, encryption, compression), Session (opening, managing and closing dialogues), Transport (end-to-end delivery between processes), Network (addressing and routing between networks), Data Link (delivery between neighbouring devices on one link) and Physical (bits on the wire or radio).

The TCP/IP model is the one the internet actually uses, and it has four layers (some books split the bottom one to make five). Application covers OSI's top three layers together. Transport is the same idea as OSI's. Internet corresponds to the Network layer and is where IP lives. Link covers Data Link and Physical; the five-layer version separates them again.

Example protocols make this concrete. Application: HTTP, DNS, SMTP. Transport: TCP, UDP. Internet/Network: IP, ICMP, and routing protocols like OSPF. Link: Ethernet and Wi-Fi (802.11), with MAC addresses. Physical: cables, signals and voltages. OSI is mostly a teaching reference; TCP/IP has no separate session or presentation layer.

Encapsulation is how data moves down the stack. When a browser sends a request, the application data gets a TCP header at the transport layer (source and destination port), making a segment. The network layer adds an IP header with source and destination addresses, making a packet. The link layer adds an Ethernet header and trailer, making a frame, and the physical layer sends the bits. The receiver reverses this, stripping one header per layer on the way up (decapsulation).$n$),
  ('Link-State vs Distance-Vector Routing', 'Contrasts OSPF-style link-state routing with RIP-style distance-vector routing, including count-to-infinity.', 'seed/routing-algorithms.md', 'course', 'nikhil.bhat@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS341A', INTERVAL '17 days', $n$Routers need the cheapest path to every network, and there are two classic ways to find it. Distance-vector routers only know what their direct neighbours tell them. Link-state routers learn the whole map and compute paths themselves.

In link-state routing, used by OSPF, each router floods a link-state advertisement (LSA) describing its own links and their costs to every other router in the area. After flooding, every router holds an identical link-state database. Each one then runs Dijkstra's shortest path algorithm from itself to build its routing table. If a link goes down, only that change is flooded and everyone reruns Dijkstra.

Distance-vector routing, used by RIP, is based on the Bellman-Ford equation: d(x,y) = min over neighbours v of c(x,v) + d(v,y). Each router keeps a table of distance to every destination and periodically sends the whole table to its neighbours only. Receiving a neighbour's table, it updates any route that becomes cheaper. RIP uses hop count, with 16 meaning unreachable.

The big weakness of distance-vector is count-to-infinity. Say B reaches network N directly and A reaches it through B. If B's link to N fails, B may hear A's advertisement of a 2-hop route, adopt it at cost 3, and then A raises its cost to 4 through B, and so on, counting up. Split horizon helps: a router never advertises a route back out of the interface it learned it from. Poison reverse goes further by advertising it with infinite cost.

Trade-offs: link-state converges quickly and avoids loops, but it needs more memory and CPU and floods more messages at first. Distance-vector is simple and light, which suits small networks, but converges slowly and can loop temporarily.$n$),
  ('What Happens During a DNS Lookup', 'Step-by-step walk through resolving www.example.com, from the stub resolver to the authoritative server, with caching and record types.', 'seed/dns-resolution.md', 'public', 'karthik.iyer@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS341A', INTERVAL '8 days', $n$DNS turns names like www.example.com into IP addresses. When you type a URL, your browser asks the stub resolver, a small client built into the operating system. The stub resolver does not do the hard work. It checks its local cache and the hosts file, and if there is no answer it forwards the question to a recursive resolver, usually run by your ISP or a public service such as 8.8.8.8 or 1.1.1.1.

The recursive resolver does the walking. If its cache is empty, it asks a root server, 'who handles .com?' The root does not know the final answer but replies with a referral to the .com TLD servers. The resolver asks a TLD server about example.com and gets another referral, this time to the authoritative name servers for example.com. Finally it asks the authoritative server for www.example.com and gets the real answer, for example an A record with an IPv4 address. It returns this to the stub resolver.

Caching is what keeps DNS fast. Every answer comes with a TTL (time to live) in seconds, and resolvers may reuse the answer until it expires. So after one lookup of example.com, the root and TLD servers are usually not bothered again. The downside is that a record change can take up to one TTL to spread, which is why people lower the TTL before a migration.

Common record types: A maps a name to an IPv4 address, AAAA to an IPv6 address, CNAME makes one name an alias of another (so www can point to the main hostname), and MX lists the mail servers for a domain with priorities. NS records say which servers are authoritative for a zone.$n$),
  ('CPU Scheduling Algorithms Compared', 'Compares FCFS, SJF, priority and round robin scheduling with a small worked waiting-time example.', 'seed/cpu-scheduling.md', 'public', 'vikram.reddy@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS342A', INTERVAL '36 days', $n$A CPU scheduler picks which ready process runs next. The common goals are low average waiting time (time spent in the ready queue), good turnaround time and a fair share for everyone. Algorithms are either non-preemptive (a process keeps the CPU until it finishes or blocks) or preemptive (the OS can take the CPU away).

FCFS (first come, first served) is the simplest: a plain queue. It suffers from the convoy effect, where many short processes get stuck behind one long CPU-bound process. SJF (shortest job first) always runs the process with the shortest next burst and gives the minimum average waiting time, but you cannot really know the next burst, so it is estimated from past bursts. Its preemptive version is SRTF (shortest remaining time first).

Priority scheduling runs the highest-priority process first. The risk is starvation: low-priority processes may wait forever. Aging fixes this by slowly raising the priority of a process the longer it waits. Round robin gives each process a fixed time quantum, then moves it to the back of the queue. If the quantum is too large it behaves like FCFS; if too small, context-switch overhead eats the CPU.

Small example with all processes arriving at time 0: P1 burst 6, P2 burst 2, P3 burst 4. FCFS order P1, P2, P3 gives the chart P1 (0-6), P2 (6-8), P3 (8-12). Waiting times (P1, P2, P3) are 0, 6 and 8, so the average is 14/3, about 4.67. SJF runs P2 (0-2), P3 (2-6), P1 (6-12). Waiting times (P1, P2, P3) are 6, 0 and 2, so the average drops to 8/3, about 2.67. Same work, much less waiting, just by putting short jobs first.$n$),
  ('Virtual Memory, Paging and Page Replacement', 'Explains paging, address translation, the TLB, page faults and common page replacement algorithms including Belady''s anomaly.', 'seed/virtual-memory-paging.md', 'public', 'rohan.gupta@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS342A', INTERVAL '26 days', $n$Virtual memory gives every process the illusion of its own large, contiguous address space. Memory is cut into fixed-size pages in the virtual address space and equal-size frames in physical RAM, commonly 4 KB each. Any page can be placed in any frame.

A virtual address is split into a page number and an offset. The page table, one per process, maps each page number to a frame number, and the offset is copied unchanged. With 4 KB pages, the low 12 bits are the offset. For example, virtual address 0x3A10 is page 3, offset 0xA10; if page 3 is in frame 7, the physical address is 0x7A10. Since the page table lives in memory, every access would cost two, so the CPU caches recent translations in the TLB (translation lookaside buffer).

With demand paging, pages are loaded only when first used. Each page table entry has a valid bit; if the page is not in memory, the access causes a page fault. The OS finds the page on disk, picks a free frame, loads it, updates the table and restarts the instruction. If there is no free frame, a page replacement algorithm picks a victim.

FIFO evicts the oldest page, LRU evicts the least recently used one, and the optimal algorithm evicts the page not needed for the longest time (needs future knowledge, so it is a benchmark only). FIFO can show Belady's anomaly: more frames can cause more faults. With the reference string 1 2 3 4 1 2 5 1 2 3 4 5, FIFO gives 9 faults with 3 frames but 10 with 4. LRU and optimal never behave this way. Thrashing happens when processes lack enough frames, so the system spends most of its time swapping pages instead of working.$n$),
  ('Deadlocks and the Banker''s Algorithm', 'The four Coffman conditions, the main ways of handling deadlock, and a worked Banker''s algorithm example.', 'seed/deadlocks-bankers.md', 'course', 'arjun.mehta@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS342A', INTERVAL '20 days', $n$A deadlock is when a set of processes each wait for a resource held by another in the set, so none can continue. Four conditions must all hold together (the Coffman conditions): mutual exclusion (a resource has one user at a time), hold and wait (holding some resources while waiting for more), no preemption (resources cannot be forcibly taken back) and circular wait (a cycle of processes each waiting on the next).

There are three approaches. Prevention breaks one Coffman condition in advance, for example by numbering resources and always requesting them in order, which kills circular wait. Avoidance grants a request only if the system stays in a safe state. Detection lets deadlock happen, finds it, then recovers by killing a process or preempting resources.

In a resource allocation graph, an edge from process to resource means a request and an edge from resource to process means the resource is assigned. If every resource has a single instance, a cycle means deadlock. With multiple instances, a cycle is necessary but not sufficient.

A state is safe if some order of running the processes lets each finish even if it asks for its maximum. The Banker's algorithm grants a request only if the resulting state is still safe. Example: 10 units of one resource; P1 holds 5 with max 8, P2 holds 2 with max 4, P3 holds 1 with max 7. Available is 10 - 8 = 2. P2 needs at most 2 more, so it runs, finishes and returns its 4 units; available becomes 4. P1 needs 3 more, so it finishes, and available becomes 9. P3 needs 6 more, so it can finish. The safe sequence is P2, P1, P3.$n$),
  ('Semaphores, Mutexes and the Producer-Consumer Problem', 'How semaphores and mutexes protect critical sections, with the bounded-buffer producer-consumer solution and its ordering pitfalls.', 'seed/semaphores-producer-consumer.md', 'public', 'aditya.joshi@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS342A', INTERVAL '11 days', $n$A race condition happens when the result depends on the exact timing of two threads touching shared data. For example, two threads both run count++, which is really load, add, store. If both load 5 before either stores, both write 6 and one update is lost. The part of code that touches shared data is the critical section, and we need mutual exclusion so only one thread is inside at a time.

A mutex is a lock with two states, locked and unlocked, and it has an owner: the thread that locked it must be the one to unlock it. A semaphore is an integer with two atomic operations. wait (P) decrements it and blocks if the value would go below zero; signal (V) increments it and wakes a waiter. A binary semaphore (0 or 1) can act like a lock, while a counting semaphore tracks a pool of identical resources, such as 5 free database connections.

In the bounded-buffer producer-consumer problem, producers put items into a buffer of size N and consumers take them out. Use three semaphores: empty = N (free slots), full = 0 (filled slots) and mutex = 1 (protects the buffer itself).
Producer: wait(empty); wait(mutex); add item; signal(mutex); signal(full).
Consumer: wait(full); wait(mutex); remove item; signal(mutex); signal(empty).

Ordering matters. If the producer swaps its first two calls and does wait(mutex) before wait(empty), then when the buffer is full it holds the mutex while blocked on empty. The consumer then cannot get the mutex to remove an item, so neither can ever proceed: deadlock. The rule is to wait on the counting semaphore first, then take the mutex, and release the mutex before signalling. Forgetting a signal leaves threads blocked forever.$n$)
) AS v(title, description, file_path, visibility, owner_email, course_code, age, content)
JOIN STUDENT s     ON s.email = v.owner_email
LEFT JOIN COURSE c ON c.course_code = v.course_code;
